{"batchcomplete":"","query":{"normalized":[{"from":"Calling_Bell","to":"Calling Bell"},{"from":"Charon's_Ashes","to":"Charon's Ashes"},{"from":"Fossilized_Helix","to":"Fossilized Helix"},{"from":"Snecko_Eye","to":"Snecko Eye"},{"from":"Art_of_War","to":"Art of War"},{"from":"Runic_Pyramid","to":"Runic Pyramid"},{"from":"Hovering_Kite","to":"Hovering Kite"},{"from":"Juzu_Bracelet","to":"Juzu Bracelet"},{"from":"Gremlin_Horn","to":"Gremlin Horn"},{"from":"Frozen_Core","to":"Frozen Core"},{"from":"Prismatic_Shard","to":"Prismatic Shard"},{"from":"Golden_Idol","to":"Golden Idol"},{"from":"Ceramic_Fish","to":"Ceramic Fish"},{"from":"Velvet_Choker","to":"Velvet Choker"},{"from":"Slaver's_Collar","to":"Slaver's Collar"},{"from":"Smiling_Mask","to":"Smiling Mask"},{"from":"Empty_Cage","to":"Empty Cage"},{"from":"Tiny_Chest","to":"Tiny Chest"}],"pages":{"2404":{"pageid":2404,"ns":0,"title":"Art of War","revisions":[{"revid":56884,"parentid":55625,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Art of War\n| Image       = ArtofWar.png\n| Rarity      = Common\n| Description = If you do not play any {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} during your turn, gain an additional {{KW|Energy}} next turn.\n| Flavor      = This ancient manuscript contains wisdom from a past age.\n| GivesToPlayer = Energy\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}\n{{R|Art of War}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Art of War}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Art of War}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Art of War}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Art of War}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Art of War}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Art of War}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Art of War}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Art of War}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Art of War}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Art of War}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Synergies ===\n* Since {{R|Art of War}} encourages you to not play {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}}, it has synergy with decks that rely on alternate ways of dealing damage:\n** Many [[Silent]] {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skills}} and {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} apply {{BD|Poison}} (such as {{C|Deadly Poison}} and {{C|Noxious Fumes}}).\n** The [[Defect]]'s [[Orbs]] can be {{KW|Channel|Channeled}} with non-attack cards (such as {{C|Zap}} and {{C|Darkness}}).\n** The [[Watcher]] can deal significant damage with {{C|Pressure Points}}, or by playing the {{C|Omega}} gained from {{C|Alpha}} and {{C|Beta}}.\n** The [[Ironclad]] has a few alternate forms of damage such as:\n*** {{C|Flame Barrier}}\n*** {{C|Combust}}\n*** {{C|Fire Breathing}}\n*** {{C|Juggernaut}}\n*** {{R|Charon's Ashes}}\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}}-heavy decks can struggle to take advantage of {{R|Art of War|Art of War's}} {{KW|Energy}} bonus.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Art of War|Art of War's}} name and appearance reference the ancient Chinese military treatise \"The Art of War\" by Sun Tzu. The Relic's function reflects the strategy of patience.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3074":{"pageid":3074,"ns":0,"title":"Calling Bell","revisions":[{"revid":55678,"parentid":50917,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Calling Bell\n|Image       = CallingBell.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Upon pickup, obtain a unique {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} and 3 relics.\n|Flavor      = This dark iron bell rang 3 times when you found it, but now stays silent.\n}}\n{{Small Card Infobox|Curse of the Bell}}\n{{R|Calling Bell}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]]. Upon pickup, it adds a {{C|Curse of the Bell}} to the player's deck and then gives them a reward of 1 random {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common relic}}, 1 random {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon relic}}, and 1 random {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare relic}}.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Calling Bell}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* The relics are always acquired ''after'' {{C|Curse of the Bell}} is added to the player's deck.\n** If {{R|Omamori}} is acquired as a reward from {{R|Calling Bell}} then it can never block {{C|Curse of the Bell}}.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Omamori}} can block the {{C|Curse of the Bell}} if it has charges remaining when {{R|Calling Bell}} is acquired.\n* {{R|Darkstone Periapt}} will give the player +6 Max HP when {{C|Curse of the Bell}} is added to their deck.\n* {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} will permanently provide the player with +1 {{BD|Strength}} since {{C|Curse of the Bell}} cannot be removed from the player's deck once acquired.\n* Effects such as {{R|Blue Candle}}, {{C|Sever Soul}}, and {{C|Second Wind}} can allow the player to {{KW|Exhaust}} the {{C|Curse of the Bell}} so that it won't be drawn again for the rest of that combat.\n* {{C|Fire Breathing}} will trigger its damage when {{C|Curse of the Bell}} is drawn.\n\n<!-- == Bugs ==\nDoes Calling Bell have the same bug as Pandora's Box where the player can avoid all the added cards?\nI suspect not since the curse is added before the relics are acquired, but I have not tested this.\n-->\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Calling Bell}} may be a reference to the [https://www.bloodborne-wiki.com/2015/03/lulling-bell.html ''Beckoning Bell''] item from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloodborne ''Bloodborne''].\n* Upon pickup, {{R|Calling Bell}} will play a bell sound.\n** When the icon for {{R|Calling Bell}} is clicked in the player's relic bar, its icon will \"flash\" and the bell sound will play again (but with a slightly randomized pitch each time).\n** This is in contrast to the relic's flavor text which states ''\"This dark iron bell rang 3 times when you found it, but now stays silent\"''. {{R|Calling Bell}} will only ring once upon pickup, and re-playing the bell sound effect in this way will play the effect no matter how many times the player clicks its icon.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Calling Bell relic reworked. (Previously gave 3 curses and 3 relics - now gives a unique Curse '{{C|Curse of the Bell}}' and 3 relics.)\n* '''Weekly Patch 49: The Designer'''\n** Can now open the Settings menu while in special card obtaining screens like from Calling Bell or All Star daily mod.\n* '''Weekly Patch 31: Custom Mode'''\n** Pride curse will no longer appear from Calling Bell and a few other sources.\n* '''Weekly Patch 21: Channel Lightning'''\n** Calling Bell can no longer give multiple campfire option relics.\n* '''Weekly Patch 20: Immolate'''\n** Potential fix for Pandora's Box and Calling Bell issues and exploits.\n* '''Weekly Patch 19: Seed'''\n** Potential fix for Calling Bell and Pandora's Box edge case issues.\n* '''Weekly Patch 18: Paranormal'''\n** Fix for Calling Bell/Library/Pandora's Box exploit.\n* '''Weekly Patch 8: Ooh Shiny!'''\n** Added logic to prevent users from accidentally skipping Calling Bell relics.\n* '''Weekly Patch 6: We're Back!'''\n** Calling Bell's relic reward screen must now be closed before proceeding. This prevents accidentally skipping them- also mimics Tiny House and Orrery's UI.\n* '''Weekly Patch 3: Balance Balance Balance'''\n** When you view the relics from Calling Bell, the proceed button is now labeled Skip Rewards, not Skip Cards.\n* '''Weekly Patch 1: The First of Many!'''\n** You can no longer open the Settings screen during a card confirmation screen (Calling Bell exploit).\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3145":{"pageid":3145,"ns":0,"title":"Cauldron","revisions":[{"revid":50952,"parentid":37134,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Cauldron\n|Image       = Cauldron.png\n|Rarity      = Shop\n|Description = When obtained, brews 5 random [[Potions|potions]].\n|Flavor      = The Merchant is actually a rather skilled potion brewer. Buy 4 get 1 free.\n}}\n{{R|Cauldron}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop}} [[Relic]]. Immediately on purchase, it grants the player 5 [[Potions]] in a normal reward screen, similar to the one used after winning a combat encounter, allowing them some flexibility on how to manage their potions.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can only be bought from the [[Merchant]]. Only 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop Relic}} is available per [[Shop]], even with {{R|The Courier}}, since it cannot restock Shop Relics.\n\n== Interactions ==\n* {{R|Cauldron}} is able to generate {{P|Fruit Juice}}, unlike {{C|Alchemize}} which cannot.\n* {{R|Cauldron}} can potentially drop the same [[Potion]] multiple times; it does not need to drop 5 ''unique'' potions.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Potion Belt}} can make it significantly easier to benefit from as many of {{R|Cauldron|Cauldron's}} [[Potions]] as possible.\n** If the player's [[Potion]] slots are empty, they can pick up all 5 of {{R|Cauldron|Cauldron's}} [[Potions]] ({{Asc|11|all but 1 of the potions}}).\n* {{R|Toy Ornithopter}} will heal 5 HP for each [[Potion]] used.\n* {{R|Sacred Bark}} will double the effect of most [[Potions]]. For a full list, see the mentions of {{R|Sacred Bark}} in [[Potions List]].\n* {{R|Cauldron}} can be somewhat more useful in the final [[Shop]] of the player's run if the other options for sale are undesirable. Even if they have no empty [[Potion]] slots, {{R|Cauldron}} may provide one or more [[Potions]] that are better than what the player currently has. It's better than entering the last [[Boss]] with ~150 ({{Asc|16|~165}}) more [[Gold]]!\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Sozu}} will render {{R|Cauldron}} completely useless.\n** Unlike buying single [[Potions]], which is prevented while the player possesses {{R|Sozu}}, {{R|Cauldron}} can still be purchased from the [[Merchant]] even though none of the [[Potions]] can be picked up. It is effectively a waste of [[Gold]].\n\n== Trivia ==\n* A cauldron is used in clich\u00e9 depictions of witchcraft for the purpose of brewing potions and other uses of black magics.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cauldron#Symbolism_and_mythology</ref>\n* The flavor text of {{R|Cauldron}} is the only reference that potentially suggests the [[Merchant]] brews all the potions they sell.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 25: Building Blocks'''\n** Cauldron and Old Coin wording improved to match other relics.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2511":{"pageid":2511,"ns":0,"title":"Ceramic Fish","revisions":[{"revid":56932,"parentid":55400,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Rename|Ceramic Fish|Lucky Fysh}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Ceramic Fish\n|Image       = CeramicFish.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Whenever you add a card to your deck, gain 9 [[Gold]].\n|Flavor      = Meticulously painted, these fish were revered to bring great fortune.\n|GivesToPlayer = Gold\n}}\n{{R|Ceramic Fish}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]]. Whenever a card is added to the player's deck, they also gain 9 [[Gold]]. This applies to ''any'' time a card is added to the deck; not just from the typical card rewards after combat. [[Transform|Transforming]] cards, purchasing cards from the [[Merchant]], cards gained from events like {{E|Match and Keep}} and {{E|The Nest}}, and the {{C|Parasite}} that [[Writhing Mass]] can permanently add to the player's deck will all trigger the '''Gold''' gain.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Ceramic Fish}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Ceramic Fish}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Ceramic Fish}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Ceramic Fish}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Ceramic Fish}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Ceramic Fish}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Ceramic Fish}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Ceramic Fish}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Ceramic Fish}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Ceramic Fish}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Prayer Wheel}} can provide more opportunities to add cards to the deck by approximately doubling the amount of card rewards offered.\n* {{R|Wing Boots}} can provide more pathing options to floors that are likely to add cards to the deck (namely [[Map Locations#Monsters|Monsters]], [[Map Locations#Elites|Elites]], and [[Map Locations#Merchant|Merchants]]).\n* {{R|Dream Catcher}} can provide more opportunities to add cards to the deck by enabling [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Rest Sites]] to also act as card rewards.\n* {{R|Bloody Idol}} will heal 5 HP when adding cards to the deck.\n* Specifically for cards purchased from the [[Merchant]], relics like {{R|The Courier}} and {{R|Membership Card}} can bring the net cost of cards down by even more.\n* The purchasing power (or \"effective gold\") at [[Merchant|Shops]] granted by {{R|Ceramic Fish}} increases if the player has {{R|The Courier}} and/or {{R|Membership Card}}.\n<!--\nBe very careful with the math if you decide rewrite this section.\nThe correct formula for purchasing power (or \"effective gold\") is [  9 / (1 - discount)  ] and NOT [  9 * (1 + discount)  ].\nA 50% discount should DOUBLE the purchasing power (+100%)! It's not just a 50% increase.\n-->\n** {{R|The Courier|The Courier's}} 20% discount makes the 9 [[Gold]] from {{R|Ceramic Fish}} have the purchasing power of 11.25 [[Gold]].\n** {{R|Membership Card|Membership Card's}} 50% discount makes the 9 [[Gold]] from {{R|Ceramic Fish}} have the purchasing power of 18 [[Gold]].\n** Having both {{R|The Courier}} and {{R|Membership Card}} is a total 60% discount. This makes the 9 [[Gold]] from {{R|Ceramic Fish}} have the purchasing power of 22.5 [[Gold]].\n\n== Trivia ==\n* As a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|common}} [[Relic]], {{R|Ceramic Fish}} will cost on average 150 gold ({{Asc|16|165 gold}}) to purchase from a [[Merchant#Prices_4|Merchant]]. This means it takes 17 cards ({{Asc|16|19 cards}}) added to the deck after purchasing {{R|Ceramic Fish}} for it to pay for itself and start generating a profit.\n* The visual design of {{R|Ceramic Fish}} may be modelled after ''Koi'' fish<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koi</ref>; a type of Amur carp<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyprinus_rubrofuscus</ref> that are selectively bred for the unique colored patterns on their scales.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** CONTENT: Common Relic: Ceramic Fish\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1444":{"pageid":1444,"ns":0,"title":"Charon's Ashes","revisions":[{"revid":56330,"parentid":55689,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Charon's Ashes\n| Image       = CharonsAshes.png\n| Rarity      = Rare\n| Character   = Ironclad\n| Description = Whenever you {{KW|Exhaust}} a card, deal 3 damage to ALL enemies.\n| Flavor      = Charon was said to be the god of rebirth, eternally dying and reviving in a burst of flame.\n| InteractsWith = Exhaust\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Exhaust}}\n{{R|Charon's Ashes}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Ironclad]] [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Charon's Ashes}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Charon's Ashes}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Charon's Ashes}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Charon's Ashes}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Charon's Ashes}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Charon's Ashes}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Charon's Ashes}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Charon's Ashes}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Charon's Ashes}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Charon's Ashes}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Charon's Ashes}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Charon's Ashes}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Cards which can {{KW|Exhaust}} multiple cards at once (such as {{C|Second Wind}}) or that draw more cards (such as {{C|Burning Pact}} and {{C|Offering}}) can be particularly useful to trigger {{R|Charon's Ashes}}.\n* {{C|Fiend Fire}} in particular is a very powerful trigger for {{R|Charon's Ashes}}; effectively turning every card in the player's hand into 3 damage to all enemies.\n* {{P|Elixir}} can be used to {{KW|Exhaust}} many cards from the player's hand.\n* Although it's a niche and rare strategy, using {{R|Prismatic Shard}} to find [[Silent]] cards which generate {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} (such as {{C|Blade Dance}} or {{C|Storm of Steel}}) can be an effective and repeatable way to trigger {{R|Charon's Ashes}} many times each turn.\n* While they don't {{KW|Exhaust}} cards directly, other {{KW|Exhaust}} synergies (such as {{C|Feel No Pain}}, {{C|Sentinel}}, and {{C|Dark Embrace}}) can make {{KW|Exhaust|Exhausting}} cards even more powerful and further incentivize taking effects which can {{KW|Exhaust}} cards.\n* Since the damage from {{R|Charon's Ashes}} is not Attack damage, it will not cause the player to take damage from {{BD|Thorns}}.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* The damage from {{R|Charon's Ashes}} will not change {{M|Writhing Mass}}' intent.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Charon is a figure from Greek mythology who acts as a guide to ferry souls of the dead into the Greek underworld.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charon</ref>\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3075":{"pageid":3075,"ns":0,"title":"Empty Cage","revisions":[{"revid":50923,"parentid":37158,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Empty Cage\n|Image       = EmptyCage.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Upon pickup, remove 2 cards from your deck.\n|Flavor      = \"How unusual to cage that which you worship.\" - Ranwid\n|Keywords    = Deck\n}}\n{{R|Empty Cage}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Empty Cage}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Empty Cage}} may be a reference to the proverb ''\"If you love something, set it free.\"'' (one of many variations of this phrase).<ref>https://quoteinvestigator.com/2012/04/08/love-set-free/</ref>\n* The flavor text ''\"How unusual to cage that which you worship.\"'' may be a reference to the cult that worships the {{M|Awakened One}} keeping birds in its likeness trapped in cages.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V1.1: The Dealer'''\n** Empty Cage relic now works with 1 or less valid cards in your deck.\n* '''Weekly Patch 29: TURBO'''\n** Adding boss relic Empty Cage.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2549":{"pageid":2549,"ns":0,"title":"Fossilized Helix","revisions":[{"revid":55792,"parentid":55192,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Fossilized Helix\n|Image       = FossilizedHelix.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = Prevent the first time you would lose HP in combat.\n|Flavor      = Seemingly indestructible, you wonder what kind of creature this belonged to.\n|Keywords    = Buffer\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Buffer}}\n{{R|Fossilized Helix}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]]. It gives the player 1 {{BD|Buffer}} at the start of each combat.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Fossilized Helix}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Fossilized Helix}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Fossilized Helix}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Fossilized Helix}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Fossilized Helix}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Fossilized Helix}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Fossilized Helix}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Fossilized Helix}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Fossilized Helix}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Fossilized Helix}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Fossilized Helix}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Fossilized Helix}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{BD|Buffer}} can be a highly effective form of damage mitigation against enemies that can attack with a single large hit early into the fight (such as {{M|Lagavulin}}, {{M|Slime Boss}}, or {{M|Shelled Parasite}})\n* Effects which help mitigate small attacks and chip damage (such as {{R|Orichalcum}}, {{C|Disarm}}, {{C|Piercing Wail}}, {{KW|Frost|Frost Orbs}}, and {{C|Talk to the Hand}}) can help block small attacks and preserve the {{BD|Buffer}} to block a much larger attack.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* There are some cards that cause the player to take a small amount of damage during their own turn, which can essentially waste the {{BD|Buffer}} before the enemies get a chance to attack the player.\n** {{C|Bloodletting}}\n** {{C|Combust}}\n** {{C|Hemokinesis}}\n** {{C|Brutality}}\n** {{C|Offering}}\n** {{C|J.A.X.}}\n** {{C|Burn}} (gained from certain enemies, or cards such as {{C|Immolate}} and {{C|Overclock}})\n** {{C|Decay}}\n** {{C|Pain}}\n** {{C|Regret}}\n* The {{M|Corrupt Heart}}'s {{BD|Beat of Death}} can remove the {{BD|Buffer}} if the player is not careful to gain {{KW|Block}} before playing other cards on their turn.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Fossilized Helix}} is a reference to the Helix Fossil from the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pok%C3%A9mon Pok\u00e9mon series].<ref>https://bulbapedia.bulbagarden.net/wiki/Helix_Fossil</ref>\n** The Helix Fossil item was popularized as an internet meme during the ''Twitch Plays Pok\u00e9mon'' run for [https://bulbapedia.bulbagarden.net/wiki/Pok%C3%A9mon_Red_and_Blue_Versions Pok\u00e9mon Red and Blue], where the audience repeatedly caused the player character to attempt to use the Helix Fossil in their inventory, despite the item doing nothing when used.<ref>https://bulbapedia.bulbagarden.net/wiki/Helix_Fossil#Trivia</ref>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Fixed {{R|Self-Forming Clay}} relic triggering incorrectly off of prevented damage from {{BD|Buffer}} power.\n* '''Weekly Patch 49: The Designer'''\n** {{BD|Painful Stabs}} power will no longer give {{C|Wound|wounds}} if the damage was prevented by {{BD|Buffer}}.\n* '''Weekly Patch 44: Chrysalis'''\n** Effects that trigger on damage like {{C|Masterful Stab}} now interacts properly with damage prevention effects like {{BD|Buffer}}.\n* '''Weekly Patch 41: Chill'''\n** New Rare Relic: Fossilized Helix.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1524":{"pageid":1524,"ns":0,"title":"Frozen Core","revisions":[{"revid":56351,"parentid":55795,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Frozen Core\n| Image       = FrozenCore.png\n| Rarity      = Boss\n| Character   = Defect\n| Description = Replaces {{R|Cracked Core}}. If you end your turn with any empty {{KW|Orb}} slots, {{KW|Channel}} 1 {{KW|Frost}}.\n| Flavor      = The crack in your core has been filled with a pulsating cold energy.\n| GivesToPlayer = Channel, Frost\n| InteractsWith = Orb\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Frost}}{{Keyword Infobox|Channel}}\n{{R|Frozen Core}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Defect|Defect-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]]. It's the upgraded form of {{R|Cracked Core}}, and replaces it entirely when acquired from a Boss Chest.\n\n== Sources ==\nCan only be obtained as a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relic}} choice, if the player has {{R|Cracked Core}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Defect]] while they possess {{R|Cracked Core}}.\n* Can never be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Order ===\n* Frozen Core will {{KW|Channel}} the {{KW|Frost|Frost Orb}} ''before'' [[Orbs#Passive|Orb Passives]] go off for the turn, so you will receive {{KW|Block}} from the new Orb.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As {{R|Frozen Core}} requires open Orb Slots to trigger, sources of additional Orb Slots can be very helpful: {{C|Capacitor}}, {{R|Runic Capacitor}}, {{R|Inserter}}, {{P|Potion of Capacity}}\n* Alternatively, manually {{KW|Evoke|Evoking}} Orbs will also free up spots: {{C|Dualcast}}, {{C|Fission}}, {{C|Multi-Cast}}\n** Although {{C|Recursion}} does Evoke an Orb manually, it also {{KW|Channel|re-Channels}} it, so no Slot is freed.\n\n==== a) General Orb Synergies ====\n* {{R|Frozen Core}} works best with other Orb sources, such as {{C|Ball Lightning}} and {{C|Darkness}}, allowing Orbs to be {{KW|Evoke|Evoked}} more frequently. <!--- No idea what to include as prototypical orb gen cards, so just went with these two--->\n* {{C|Blizzard}} does more damage for each {{KW|Frost|Frost Orb}} {{KW|Channel|Channelled}} this combat.\n* Sources of {{BD|Focus}} will improve the effects of Orbs: {{C|Defragment}}, {{C|Consume}}, {{C|Biased Cognition}}, {{R|Data Disk}}\n* {{C|Compile Driver}} draws a card for each unique type of Orb {{KW|Channel|Channeled}}.\n* {{C|Barrage}} deals damage for each {{KW|Channel|Channeled}} Orb.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{C|Reprogram}} and {{C|Hyperbeam}} give negative {{BD|Focus}}, making Orbs generated by Frozen Core less effective.\n* Cards that generate a lot of Orbs quickly (such as {{C|Chill}}, {{C|Rainbow}}, and {{C|Tempest}}) can render {{R|Frozen Core}} essentially useless as there are no open Orb slots for a {{KW|Frost|Frost Orb}} to be {{KW|Channel|channelled}} into.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 44: Chrysalis'''\n** WORDING: Frozen Core description coloring is now consistent with other starter replacement boss relics.\n* '''Weekly Patch 33: Terror'''\n** Adding relic Frozen Core.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3207":{"pageid":3207,"ns":0,"title":"Golden Idol","revisions":[{"revid":37173,"parentid":23681,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"''This is a disambiguation page.''\n\n----\n\nThere are two pages in this wiki named Golden Idol.\n\nGo to {{R|Golden Idol|Golden Idol (Relic)}} for the Relic.\n\nGo to {{E|Golden Idol}} [[Golden Idol (Event)|(Event)]] for the Event.\n\n__DISAMBIG__\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Events]]\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2514":{"pageid":2514,"ns":0,"title":"Gremlin Horn","revisions":[{"revid":55805,"parentid":50843,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Gremlin Horn\n|Image       = GremlinHorn.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = Whenever an enemy dies, gain 1 {{KW|Energy}} and draw 1 card.\n|Flavor      = \"Gremlin Nobs are capable of growing until the day they die. Remarkable.\" - Ranwid\n|Keywords    = Draw\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}\n{{R|Gremlin Horn}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Gremlin Horn}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Gremlin Horn}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Gremlin Horn}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Gremlin Horn}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Gremlin Horn}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Gremlin Horn}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Gremlin Horn}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Gremlin Horn}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Gremlin Horn}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Gremlin Horn}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Gremlin Horn}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* If an enemy dies on ''their'' turn (e.g. by {{BD|Poison}} or attacking into {{BD|Thorns}}), {{R|Gremlin Horn}} will still trigger its extra {{KW|Energy}} and card draw. However, without {{R|Ice Cream}}, energy is reset to its current maximum at the start of the player's turn so the extra energy will be effectively lost.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Gremlin Horn|Gremlin Horn's}} flavor text references [[Gremlin Nob]]; an [[Elite]] enemy that can be fought in [[Acts|Act]] 1, or in [[Acts|Act]] 2 during {{E|The Colosseum}} event. It looks very similar to the '''Gremlin Nob''''s horns as well.\n* It's possible that {{R|Gremlin Horn}} could be a horn that was broken off of [[Gremlin Leader]] as they are a Gremlin and their left horn is visibly broken.\n** There is precedent for relics having previously belonged to enemies, such as {{R|Champion Belt}} belonging to [[The Champ]] and {{R|Bronze Scales}} having fallen off of [[The Guardian]]'s {{BD|Sharp Hide}}. {{R|Gremlin Horn}} makes no such direct reference, and is therefore speculative.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2394":{"pageid":2394,"ns":0,"title":"Hovering Kite","revisions":[{"revid":55443,"parentid":50942,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Hovering Kite\n|Image       = HoveringKite.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Character   = Silent\n|Description = The first time you discard a card each turn, gain 1 {{KW|Energy}}.\n|Flavor      = The Kite floats around you in battle, propelled by a mysterious force.\n|Keywords    = Discard\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}{{R|Hovering Kite}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Silent|Silent-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relic}}.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Hovering Kite}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Silent]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Hovering Kite}} will activate when [[Cards|cards]] are [[Discard|discarded]] in any of the following ways:\n\n==== Cards ====\n* Many {{QueryLink|Cards|color:Green|Silent}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} and {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skills}} will [[Discard|discard]] 1 or more [[Cards|cards]] when played. \n** {{C|Survivor}}\n** {{C|Acrobatics}}\n** {{C|Dagger Throw}}\n** {{C|Prepared}}\n** {{C|All-Out Attack}}\n** {{C|Concentrate}}\n** {{C|Calculated Gamble}}\n** {{C|Unload}}\n** {{C|Storm of Steel}}\n* {{C|Tools of the Trade}} will [[Discard|discard]] 1 [[card]] at the start of each turn.\n* {{C|Scrape}}, if the player is able to obtain a copy through {{R|Prismatic Shard}} or {{E|A Note For Yourself}}.\n\n==== Relics and potions ====\n* {{R|Gambling Chip}}\n* {{P|Gambler's Brew}}\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher''' (01-14-2020)\n** GAMEPLAY: Hovering Kite relic reworked.\n*** Previously: Gain 1 {{KW|Energy}} and discard 2 [[Cards|cards]] at the start of each turn.\n*** Now: The first time you [[Discard|discard]] a [[card]] each turn, gain 1 {{KW|Energy}}.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2523":{"pageid":2523,"ns":0,"title":"Juzu Bracelet","revisions":[{"revid":55447,"parentid":50811,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Juzu Bracelet\n|Image       = JuzuBracelet.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Regular enemy combats are no longer encountered in ? rooms.\n|Flavor      = A ward against the unknown.\n}}\n{{R|Juzu Bracelet}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]]. See also [[Unknown Location]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Juzu Bracelet}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Juzu Bracelet}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Juzu Bracelet}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Juzu Bracelet}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Juzu Bracelet}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Juzu Bracelet}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Juzu Bracelet}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Juzu Bracelet}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Juzu Bracelet}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Juzu Bracelet}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n* Will not spawn when using the [[Custom Mode]] modifier {{KW|Deadly Events}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Juzu Bracelet relic no longer spawns beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 5: Happy Holidays!'''\n** Wording update for Juzu bracelet to specify regular monster encounters (as events can have enemies).\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3135":{"pageid":3135,"ns":0,"title":"Necronomicon","revisions":[{"revid":55867,"parentid":51297,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Necronomicon\n|Image       = Necronomicon.png\n|Rarity      = Event\n|Source      = Cursed Tome\n|Description = The first {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} played each turn that costs 2 or more is played twice. Upon pickup, obtain a special {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}}.\n|Flavor      = Only a fool would try and harness this evil power. At night your dreams are haunted by images of the book devouring your mind.\n}}\n{{Small Card Infobox|Necronomicurse}}\n{{R|Necronomicon}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Event|Event}} [[Relic]]. The \"special {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}}\" that it adds to the player's deck is {{C|Necronomicurse}}.\n\n== Sources ==\nCan only be obtained from {{E|Cursed Tome}} after choosing the {{Frame|Read}} -> {{Frame|Continue}} -> {{Frame|Continue}} -> {{Frame|Continue}} -> {{Frame|Take}} options and randomly receiving {{R|Necronomicon}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* If an X-Cost {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} ({{C|Whirlwind}} and {{C|Skewer}}) is played while spending at least 2 {{KW|Energy}}, it will be played twice. Both plays will use the original value of X for its effect, even though the player will likely have 0 {{KW|Energy}} the second time it's played.\n** For example, if the player spends 3 {{Icon|GE}}[[Energy]] on {{C|Skewer}}, it will be played twice and both plays will deal 7 damage '''3''' times (a total of 6 hits). The player will be left with 0 {{Icon|GE}}[[Energy]] remaining as expected.\n* Many {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} that cost 2 or more are very powerful cards. Cards such as {{C|Immolate}}, {{C|Predator}}, {{C|Sunder}}, and {{C|Wallop}} can greatly benefit from being played twice without needing to pay an additional {{KW|Energy}} cost.\n* {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} gains an effectively permanent +1 {{BD|Strength}} since the {{C|Necronomicurse}} can't easily be removed from the player's deck.\n* {{R|Darkstone Periapt}} will give the player +6 Max HP if they possess it before acquiring {{R|Necronomicon}}.\n* Despite {{C|Necronomicurse}} stating that ''\"There is no escape from this curse\"'', {{R|Omamori}} can block the {{C|Necronomicurse}} if it has charges remaining when {{R|Necronomicon}} is acquired.\n** The behavior of {{C|Necronomicurse}} stating that ''\"There is no escape from this curse\"'' does actually have other implications that separate it from cards that simply ''\"Cannot be removed from your deck\"'' (such as {{C|Ascender's Bane}} and {{C|Curse of the Bell}}). See the page on {{C|Necronomicurse}} for more information.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* If the player loses possession of {{R|Necronomicon}} in some way (such as by trading it for {{R|N'loth's Gift}} in the {{E|N'loth}} [[Event]]), the {{C|Necronomicurse}} will also be removed from the player's deck.\n* Both plays of the card will be played on the same target.\n** If the target dies before the 2nd play occurs, it will effectively be wasted and {{R|Necronomicon}} will not play another {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} twice for the rest of that turn.\n* {{R|Necronomicon}} only cares about the ''current'' cost of an {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} being 2 or more, and pays no attention to the ''original'' cost of the card.\n** For example, if {{C|Wheel Kick}} is made 0-cost by playing {{C|Swivel}}, then {{R|Necronomicon}} '''will not''' play it twice.\n** On the other hand, if {{R|Snecko Eye}} causes {{C|Die Die Die}} to cost 2 or more, then {{R|Necronomicon}} '''will''' play it twice.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Necronomicon}} is a reference to the Necronomicon (or \"the book of the dead\"); a fictional grimoire from the writings of gothic horror author H.P. Lovecraft.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Necronomicon</ref>\n** The Necronomicon is an iconic item in gothic horror, and is referenced in other media franchises such as [https://spelunky.fandom.com/wiki/Book_of_the_Dead_(HD) Spelunky] and [https://evildead.fandom.com/wiki/Necronomicon_Ex-Mortis Evil Dead].\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2486":{"pageid":2486,"ns":0,"title":"Prismatic Shard","revisions":[{"revid":55908,"parentid":50969,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Prismatic Shard\n|Image       = PrismaticShard.png\n|Rarity      = Shop\n|Description = Combat reward screens now contain {{QueryLink|Cards|color:Colorless|Colorless}} cards and cards from other colors.\n|Flavor      = Looking through the shard, you are able to see entirely new perspectives.\n}}\n{{R|Prismatic Shard}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop}} [[Relic]]. It allows all card rewards to contain {{QueryLink|Cards|color:Colorless|Colorless}} cards, as well as colored cards from other characters. Cards from the player character's normal color can still be presented in card rewards, although it is now much less likely as the card pool is heavily diluted.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can only be bought from the [[Merchant]]. Only 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop Relic}} is available per [[Shop]], even with {{R|The Courier}}, since it cannot restock Shop Relics.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Cannot be obtained with {{KW|Diverse}} enabled in the [[Custom Mode|Daily Climb]] as they both have the same effect.\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Order ===\n* If the player is not playing as the [[Defect]], they will gain 1 {{KW|Orb Slot}} at the start of each combat.\n\n=== Finding Cards of Other Colors ===\nOnly card rewards that would permanently add a card to the player's deck are affected by {{R|Prismatic Shard}}. Effects will generally mention ''\"obtain a card\"'' or specifically adding a card ''\"to your deck\"'' when they acts as card rewards, while effects that '''don't''' act as card rewards will always be phrased like ''\"choose 1 of 3 cards\"'' while in combat.\n\nFor completeness, all scenarios that seem like they could reasonably contain cards of any color are listed in the sections below depending on whether they are affected by {{R|Prismatic Shard}} or not.\n\n==== Affected Card Rewards ====\nDespite {{R|Prismatic Shard}}'s description saying otherwise, '''ALL''' card rewards that do not specify a particular color will be affected by {{R|Prismatic Shard}} and can show the player cards of any color.\n\n* End-of-combat card rewards from defeating [[Monsters|normal enemies]].\n* End-of-combat card rewards from defeating [[Elites|elites]].\n* The guaranteed {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare}} card reward from defeating a [[Map Locations#Boss|Boss]] at the end of [[Acts|Act]] 1 and 2.\n* {{R|Dream Catcher}}'s card reward after resting at a [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Rest Site]].\n* {{R|Tiny House}}'s card reward after acquiring it.\n* {{R|Prayer Wheel}}'s extra card reward from defeating [[Monsters|normal enemies]].\n* {{R|Orrery}}'s card rewards after acquiring it.\n\n==== Unaffected Card Rewards ====\nThe following scenarios where cards can be acquired are '''not''' affected by {{R|Prismatic Shard}}:\n\n* {{E|Sensory Stone}}'s 1-3 card rewards, since it specifies that only {{QueryLink|Cards|color:Colorless|Colorless}} cards are offered.\n* {{C|Foreign Influence}}'s card reward when played (it also already shows the player {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} of any color and {{QueryLink|Cards|color:Colorless&type:Attack|Colorless Attacks}}).\n* {{C|Discovery}}'s card reward when played.\n* {{R|Toolbox}}'s card reward at the start of combat (it also specifies that only {{QueryLink|Cards|color:Colorless|Colorless}} cards are offered).\n* {{R|Nilry's Codex}}'s card reward at the end of each turn in combat.\n* {{P|Attack Potion}}, {{P|Skill Potion}}, and {{P|Power Potion}}'s card rewards when consumed.\n* Cards offered by {{E|The Library}} and cards for sale at the [[Merchant]] are unaffected because they are not card rewards.\n\n=== Synergies ===\nEach character's card pools are balanced assuming they will only have access to their own card pool and the {{QueryLink|Cards|color:Colorless|Colorless}} cards. For this reason, {{R|Prismatic Shard}} can allow the player to create very powerful combos using cards from multiple characters which would normally never be possible. The only real limits are the player's luck and imagination, but listed below are some of the simpler combinations and synergies:\n\n<!--\nNOTE: Don't go too overboard with this list; coming up with new ideas is fun but we also don't want a super long 50-item list that contains everyone's favourite multi-color combos in it.\nUse great restraint when adding to this list; focus on colors that are under-represented!\n-->\n* {{C|Juggernaut}} plus {{C|After Image}} will cause '''every''' card played to also deal 5 (7) damage to a random enemy.\n** Similarly, {{C|Juggernaut}} plus {{KW|Frost|Frost Orbs}} will deal 5 (7) damage to a random enemy each time the orbs trigger. Particularly effective if the player can also find a few copies of {{C|Capacitor}}.\n* {{C|Piercing Wail}} and {{C|Wraith Form}} can make the drawbacks of {{R|Brimstone}} and {{C|Berserk}} significantly more manageable.\n* {{C|Reprogram}} can be used to great effect by any deck that relies on dealing damage mostly from {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}}, and/or mostly mitigates damage from {{KW|Block}}, and makes little to no use of {{KW|Orb|Orbs}}.\n* {{C|Master Reality}} can be used to {{KW|Upgrade}} cards duplicated by {{C|Dual Wield}} and {{C|Nightmare}}, {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} from {{C|Blade Dance}} and {{C|Storm of Steel}}, and the random cards from {{C|Infernal Blade}}, {{C|Distraction}}, {{C|White Noise}}, {{C|Hello World}}, and {{C|Creative AI}}.\n* {{C|Flex}}, {{C|Inflame}}, {{C|Spot Weakness}}, and {{C|Limit Break}} can greatly improve the damage of decks that rely on using cards such as {{C|Blade Dance}}, {{C|Storm of Steel}}, {{C|All for One}}, {{C|Flurry of Blows}}, and {{C|Weave}} to play many cheap {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}}.\n* Every character can now find near-universally powerful cards such as {{C|Disarm}}, {{C|Impervious}}, {{C|Piercing Wail}}, {{C|Acrobatics}}, {{C|Wraith Form}}, {{C|TURBO}}, {{C|Self Repair}}, {{C|Echo Form}}, {{C|Vault}}, and {{C|Scrawl}}.\n\n=== Anti Synergies ===\n* Because card rewards become heavily diluted with almost every card in the game, it becomes ''significantly'' more difficult to find specific cards or support character-specific synergies and synergy payoffs (such as [[Ironclad]]'s {{KW|Exhaust}}, [[Silent]]'s {{BD|Poison}}, [[Defect]]'s {{KW|Orb|Orbs}}, or [[Watcher]]'s {{KW|Stance|Stances}} and {{BD|Mantra}}).\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The visual design of {{R|Prismatic Shard}} is a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dispersive_prism dispersive prism]; a flat, polished piece of a transparent material formed into a prism which separates white light into its component colors. In other words, it's a prism made of special glass that turns normal white light into all the colours of the rainbow.\n** This is the inspiration for the mechanical effect of {{R|Prismatic Shard}}, as it enables the player to find cards of every color where previously they could only find their character's specific color.\n* {{R|Prismatic Shard}} is likely a reference to the [https://stardewvalleywiki.com/Prismatic_Shard item of the same name] from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stardew_Valley Stardew Valley].\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Slay the Spire 1.0: Farewell Early Access'''\n** Fixed issue where Prismatic Shard can return cards which were not yet unlocked.\n* '''Weekly Patch 56: The Collector'''\n** Fixed issue where card rewards via Prismatic Shard could contain duplicates.\n* '''Weekly Patch 54: Loop'''\n** Potential fix for Prismatic Shard returning duplicate cards on rare occasions.\n* '''Weekly Patch 52: Release Date Price Increase'''\n** Duplicate cards will no longer appear on reward screens with Prismatic Shard.\n* '''Weekly Patch 47: Metamorphosis'''\n** Prismatic Shard now sets your max orbs to 1 only if you have 0 max orbs and you aren't the Defect.\n* '''Weekly Patch 43: Discovery'''\n** Prismatic Shard now gives 1 empty orb slot to non-Defect characters.\n** Prismatic Shard relic no longer spawns when the Diverse daily is activated.\n* '''Weekly Patch 42: Reboot'''\n** Updating anti-dupe logic for card rewards for Prismatic Shard relic.\n* '''Weekly Patch 41: Chill'''\n* * New Shop Relic: Prismatic Shard.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2402":{"pageid":2402,"ns":0,"title":"Runic Pyramid","revisions":[{"revid":50931,"parentid":37249,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Runic Pyramid\n|Image       = RunicPyramid.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = At the end of your turn, you no longer discard your hand.\n|Flavor      = The runes are indecipherable.\n|Keywords    = Discard, Hand\n}}\n{{R|Runic Pyramid}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Runic Pyramid}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Runic Pyramid}} can improve effects that scale with the number of cards in the player's hand. Examples include:\n** An {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} {{C|Armaments|Armaments+}}\n** {{C|Fiend Fire}}\n** {{C|Calculated Gamble}}\n** {{C|Storm of Steel}}\n** {{C|Spirit Shield}}\n** {{R|Cloak Clasp}}\n* Cards that can be difficult to play due to their high {{KW|Energy}} cost (such as {{C|Demon Form}}, {{C|Echo Form}}, and {{C|Wish}}) can be kept in hand until an appropriate turn where it can be played safely.\n* Situational cards (such as {{C|Spot Weakness}}, {{C|Piercing Wail}}, {{C|Reinforced Body}}, and {{C|Panic Button}}) can be kept in hand until they can be used to maximum effect.\n* Cards with {{KW|Fatal}} ({{C|Feed}}, {{C|Lesson Learned}}, and {{C|Ritual Dagger}}) can be kept in hand until they are guaranteed to kill a non-[[Minions|minion]] enemy.\n* {{R|Medical Kit}} can be used to {{KW|Exhaust}} any {{KW|Unplayable}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Status|Statuses}} that would otherwise fill up the player's hand.\n* {{R|Blue Candle}} can be used to {{KW|Exhaust}} any {{KW|Unplayable}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} that would otherwise fill up the player's hand.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{R|Runic Pyramid|Runic Pyramid's}} effect is distinct from {{KW|Retain}}, despite behaving essentially the same. This means {{C|Establishment}} will not discount all cards in hand at the end of the turn. It will, however, continue to discount cards that are specifically ''{{KW|Retain|Retained}}'' as normal.\n* {{KW|Unplayable}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Status|Statuses}} and {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} with an end-of-turn effect will still discard themselves at the end of the turn. They are {{C|Burn}}, {{C|Decay}}, {{C|Doubt}}, {{C|Regret}}, and {{C|Shame}}.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* Any {{KW|Unplayable}} card, such as {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Status|Statuses}} and {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} that do not have an end-of-turn effect, will not discard at the end of the player's turn. This can result in the player's hand filling with unplayable cards.\n** Enemies which add {{KW|Unplayable}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Status|Statuses}} into the player's deck can be particularly deadly, such as {{M|Taskmaster}} and {{M|Book of Stabbing}}.\n** Cards like {{C|Pain}} and {{C|Normality}} can be particularly devastating if the player has no way to remove them from their hand (for example, via {{KW|Exhaust}} or [[Discard]] effects).\n* Effects that {{KW|Retain}}, such as {{C|Equilibrium}} or {{C|Sands of Time}}, are made somewhat redundant, save for how they can continue to be discounted by {{C|Establishment}}.\n** {{C|Well-Laid Plans}} becomes especially redundant, as {{KW|Retain|Retaining}} cards is its only effect, save for somewhat uncommon scenarios like triggering {{R|Orange Pellets}} or {{R|Mummified Hand}}.\n* It can be difficult for the player to empty their hand, and therefore difficult to trigger {{R|Unceasing Top}}, since they will no longer discard any unplayed cards at the end of their turn.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The runes in {{R|Runic Pyramid}} can be seen on the right side of {{R|Stone Calendar}}.\n* {{R|Runic Pyramid}} is part of the \"runic relic set\" alongside {{R|Runic Capacitor}}, {{R|Runic Cube}}, and {{R|Runic Dome}}. Unlike most other \"relic sets\", the runic relics have no common mechanic and are purely related by name and visual design.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 28: The Ritual'''\n** Runic Pyramid relic no longer has a downside. (was previously -1 card per turn)\n* '''Weekly Patch 3: Balance Balance Balance'''\n** Don't prompt for Well Laid Plans if you have Runic Pyramid.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3082":{"pageid":3082,"ns":0,"title":"Slaver's Collar","revisions":[{"revid":55943,"parentid":50933,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Slaver's Collar\n|Image       = SlaversCollar.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = During Boss and Elite combats, gain 1 {{KW|Energy}} at the start of your turn.\n|Flavor      = Rusty miserable chains.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}{{R|Slaver's Collar}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Slaver's Collar}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n{{R|Slaver's Collar}} will provide extra {{KW|Energy}} in ''ALL'' [[Boss]] and [[Elite]] combats, including the following [[Events]] where [[Bosses]] and [[Elites]] can be fought:\n* The potential [[Elite]] combat after selecting {{Frame|Search}} in {{E|Dead Adventurer}}.\n* The [[Elite]] combat after selecting {{Frame|VICTORY}} in {{E|The Colosseum}}.\n* The [[Boss]] combat after selecting {{Frame|I am War}} in {{E|Mind Bloom}}.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As with all {{KW|Energy}} relics, {{R|Slaver's Collar}} can allow the player to play high-impact expensive cards more easily (during [[Boss]] and [[Elite]] combats).\n* {{R|Sling of Courage}} grants the player +2 {{BD|Strength}} in [[Elite]] combats, and can allow {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}}-heavy decks to kill [[Elites]] more easily.\n* {{R|Preserved Insect}} reduces the HP of [[Elites]] by 25%, allowing them to be killed sooner. This can have a compounding effect with {{R|Slaver's Collar}}, potentially allowing these fights to end even sooner.\n* {{R|Black Star}} makes fighting [[Elites]] more desirable, and the extra {{KW|Energy}} from {{R|Slaver's Collar}} can make these fights easier to win.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2533":{"pageid":2533,"ns":0,"title":"Smiling Mask","revisions":[{"revid":50824,"parentid":37257,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Smiling Mask\n|Image       = SmilingMask.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = The merchant's card removal service now always costs 50 [[Gold]].\n|Flavor      = Mask worn by the merchant. He must have spares...\n}}\n{{R|Smiling Mask}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Smiling Mask}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Smiling Mask}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Smiling Mask}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Smiling Mask}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Smiling Mask}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Smiling Mask}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Smiling Mask}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Smiling Mask}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Smiling Mask}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Smiling Mask}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n* Cannot be bought from the [[Merchant]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n* Price-reduction effects (such as {{R|The Courier}} and {{R|Membership Card}}) will not affect the price of card removals if the player possesses {{R|Smiling Mask}}. It will always set the price of the card removal service to 50 [[Gold]] even if its price would otherwise be lower.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Smiling Mask}} is identical to the mask worn by the [[Merchant]], and this is referenced in the relic's flavor text.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 54: Loop'''\n** Smiling Mask relic stops flashing if Card Removal Service has been utilized.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Smiling Mask relic no longer spawns beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 47: Metamorphosis'''\n** Smiling Mask relic will no longer spawn on Hoarder daily mod.\n* '''Weekly Patch 3: Balance Balance Balance'''\n** Smiling Mask relic is now Uncommon (was Rare).\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2320":{"pageid":2320,"ns":0,"title":"Snecko Eye","revisions":[{"revid":55948,"parentid":50934,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Snecko Eye\n|Image       = SneckoEye.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = At the start of your turn, draw 2 additional cards. Start each combat {{BD|Confused}}.\n|Flavor      = An eye of a fallen snecko. Much larger than you imagined.\n|Keywords    = Draw\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Confused}}{{R|Snecko Eye}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]]. The player will draw 2 additional cards every turn while it is in their possession. At the start of combat, it grants the player the {{BD|Confused}} [[Debuff]], which randomizes the cost of any drawn card between 0 and 3 {{KW|Energy}} (inclusive).\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Snecko Eye}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Snecko Eye}} always applies {{BD|Confused}} before {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}} grants {{BD|Artifact}}, no matter the order those relics were obtained.\n\n=== Average Cost ===\nAs a general note, these figures can be somewhat misleading since {{BD|Confused}} pays no attention to the \"normal\" cost of a card when it's randomized. Players may find it more useful to almost exclusively consider the ''effect'' of a card, and largely disregard its ''normal cost'', when deciding whether to add it to their deck or not.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n|+ Card Cost Chances While Confused\n|-\n! Normal Cost !! Chance of Becoming Cheaper !! Chance of Becoming More Expensive\n|-\n| 0 || 0% || 75%\n|-\n| 1 || 25% || 50%\n|-\n| 2 || 50% || 25%\n|-\n| 3 || 75% || 0%\n|}\n\n* The average cost of cards while {{BD|Confused}} is 1.5 {{KW|Energy}}.\n* There are multiple ways to interpret the chance of a card becoming a certain cost. For example, 1-cost cards can also be thought of as having a 50% chance to ''not become more expensive''. In other words, 1-cost cards have a 50% chance to cost either 1 or 0 (same cost or cheaper), and a 50% chance to cost either 2 or 3 (more expensive).\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Drawing 2 additional cards every turn for the rest of the run is a '''very''' powerful effect. Focusing more on generating {{KW|Energy}} (through effects such as {{C|Bloodletting}}, {{C|Adrenaline}}, {{C|Recycle}}, {{KW|Calm}}, and {{R|Mummified Hand}}) can be an effective way to play the higher volume of cards drawn each turn.\n** Reliable {{KW|Energy}} generation can also help manage turns where {{R|Snecko Eye}} has mostly randomized cards to be expensive.\n* {{BD|Confused}} changes the cost of a card, thus any effect that cares about card cost is affected. This includes {{C|Recycle}}, {{C|All for One}}, {{R|Wrist Blade}}, and {{R|Necronomicon}}.\n* {{C|Corruption}} overrides {{BD|Confused}}, so {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skills}} will always cost 0 while affected by both {{BD|Corruption}} and {{BD|Confused}}.\n* {{C|Swivel}} also overrides {{BD|Confused}}, but only until an {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} is played. Drawn {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} still have their cost randomized, but the player won't be able to see their cost until they spend their {{BD|Free Attack Power}}.\n* Copies generated with {{C|Dual Wield}} or {{C|Nightmare}} will retain the randomized cost of the card that was originally copied. For example, duplicating a card that costs 0 will create additional 0-cost copies.\n* Since effects like {{C|Hologram}} and {{P|Liquid Memories}} don't count as drawing, any card retrieved from the player's discard pile will '''not''' have its cost randomized again when it enters their hand.\n** If the player ends up with two 0-cost {{KW|Upgrade|upgraded}} {{C|Hologram|Holograms}} in hand, the player may play them endlessly to generate as much {{KW|Block}} as they want (or trigger {{R|Letter Opener}} infinitely) since their cost will remain 0 when fetched from the discard pile.\n** Everything that applies to {{C|Hologram}} also applies to {{C|All for One}}, except the player will move ALL 0-cost cards from their discard pile to their hand.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{KW|Unplayable}} and X-Cost cards are unaffected by {{BD|Confused}}.\n* {{R|Orange Pellets}} may be used to remove {{BD|Confused}}. Cards that already have their cost randomized will keep the same randomized cost for the rest of the combat.\n* {{BD|Confused}} only affects cards that are '''''drawn''''' into the player's hand via an effect that is explicitly stated as a card draw effect. Any effect that states a card is '''''put''''' into the hand (such as from {{C|Seek}}, {{C|Secret Technique}}, {{C|Secret Weapon}}, or {{C|Violence}}) will not have their cost changed since they are not card draw effects.\n* Cards created during combat (such as the {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} gained by playing {{C|Blade Dance}}) won't have their cost randomized unless they are shuffled into the draw pile and drawn again.\n* {{BD|Confused}} will overwrite the current cost of {{C|Blood for Blood}}, {{C|Masterful Stab}}, and {{C|Force Field}}. However, these cards will reduce/increase their cost as usual until drawn again.\n* If a card changes its cost if it is {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}}, then it can be {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} in combat (such as from {{C|Armaments}} or {{P|Blessing of the Forge}}) to set its cost to the {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} value. This can be used to \"fix\" expensive cards that could be made cheaper. This does not work if the card is already {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} when its cost is randomized to an expensive value.\n** Examples include {{C|Seeing Red}}, {{C|Alchemize}}, {{C|Recursion}}, and {{C|Eruption}}.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Runic Pyramid}} prevents cards being discarded at the end of the player's turn, which may result in their hand getting clogged with expensive cards.\n** Likewise, {{KW|Retain|Retaining}} cards will likely get stuck in the player's hand if they roll a high cost.\n* {{BD|Confused}} negates the {{KW|Energy}} discounts of {{C|Setup}} and {{C|Forethought}}. It also negates the {{KW|Energy}} discount of cards like {{C|Madness}} once the card is drawn again.\n* {{BD|Confused}} will randomize the cost of '''any''' card which is ''not {{KW|Unplayable}}'' and has an {{KW|Energy}} cost. This includes cards like {{C|Slimed}} and {{C|Pride}}, which can be made more expensive and therefore more difficult to play so that they {{KW|Exhaust}}.\n\n== Bugs ==\n* {{C|Eviscerate}} has many bugged interactions with {{R|Snecko Eye}}. More info [[Eviscerate#With_Snecko_Eye|here]].\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Snecko Eye}} used to belong to one of the {{M|Snecko}} enemies that the player can fight in Act 2.\n* [[Snecko Skull#Trivia|Unlike Snecko Skull]] (the other relic that references the {{M|Snecko}} enemy), {{R|Snecko Eye}} actually does provide the player with an effect that is directly used by the {{M|Snecko}} in combat; that being the {{BD|Confused}} debuff.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 49: The Designer'''\n** Minor Snecko Eye description fix.\n* '''Weekly Patch 47: Metamorphosis'''\n** Fix for a scenario which upgrading cards affected by Snecko Eye would sometimes give negative cost cards.\n* '''Weekly Patch 29: TURBO'''\n** We added special runs that you can play by entering specific seeds:\n** PRAISESNECK0 Will replace your starting relic with Snecko Eye.\n* '''Weekly Patch 11: A Library of Features'''\n** Enchiridion now works with Snecko Eye properly.\n* '''Weekly Patch 3: Balance Balance Balance'''\n** New boss relic: Snecko Eye.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2538":{"pageid":2538,"ns":0,"title":"Tiny Chest","revisions":[{"revid":50827,"parentid":37276,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Tiny Chest\n|Image       = TinyChest.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Every 4th ? room is a Treasure room.\n|Flavor      = \"A fine prototype.\" - The Architect\n}}\n{{R|Tiny Chest}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|Common:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Tiny Chest}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Tiny Chest}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Tiny Chest}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Tiny Chest}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Tiny Chest}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Tiny Chest}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Tiny Chest}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Tiny Chest}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Tiny Chest}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Tiny Chest}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 36 (floor 2 of [[Acts#Act 3: The City|Act 3]]). Since the first floor of each Act is always a [[Map Locations#Monsters|normal enemy fight]], this effectively means that {{R|Tiny Chest}} cannot be obtained after leaving [[Act 2]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Tiny Chest}} can allow the extra relics provided by {{R|Matryoshka}} to be obtained sooner than normal thanks to its extra [[Map Locations#Treasure Room|Treasure Room]] generation.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* Encountering more chests can be awkward if the player has {{R|Cursed Key}} since it will add a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} to the player's deck (unless the player also has {{R|Omamori}} to prevent gaining the curse).\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Tiny Chest relic reworked.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Tiny Chest relic no longer spawns beyond floor 40 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 3: Balance Balance Balance'''\n** Small adjustment to Tiny Chest logic.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2494":{"pageid":2494,"ns":0,"title":"Velvet Choker","revisions":[{"revid":50937,"parentid":37288,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Velvet Choker\n|Image       = VelvetChoker.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Gain 1 {{KW|Energy}} at the start of your turn. You cannot play more than 6 cards per turn.\n|Flavor      = \"Immense power, but too limited.\" - Kublai the Great\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}{{R|Velvet Choker}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Velvet Choker}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As with all {{KW|Energy}} relics, {{R|Velvet Choker}} can allow the player to play high-impact expensive cards more easily.\n* The drawback of {{R|Velvet Choker}} can be somewhat more manageable if the player finds they are mostly playing a relatively small amount of high-impact cards each turn. For example:\n** Mostly dealing damage with cards like {{C|Immolate}}, {{C|Riddle with Holes}}, {{C|Hyperbeam}} or {{C|Multi-Cast}}, and {{C|Ragnarok}}.\n** Mostly mitigating damage with cards like {{C|Impervious}}, {{C|Leg Sweep}} or {{C|Wraith Form}}, {{C|Glacier}}, and {{C|Wallop}}.\n** Mostly finding scaling to keep up with later [[Elites]] and [[Bosses]] with cards like {{C|Demon Form}}, {{C|Bouncing Flask}} and {{C|Catalyst}}, {{C|Consume}} or {{C|Biased Cognition}}, and {{C|Blasphemy}}.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Velvet Choker}}'s counter will increase for ''every'' card played, even if it is just a copy and not directly played from hand. Effects such as {{C|Havoc}}, {{C|Double Tap}}, {{C|Burst}}, {{C|Amplify}}, {{C|Echo Form}}, {{C|Omniscience}}, and {{P|Duplication Potion}} will all count as multiple card plays.\n* {{R|Velvet Choker}} effectively removes the ability to opt into infinite combos (such as the [[Sundial#Bugs|Sundial infinite combo]]).\n* Effects which encourage lots of cards being played are more difficult to use at their fullest potential, such as {{C|Corruption}} (especially when combined with {{C|Dark Embrace}} and/or {{R|Dead Branch}}), {{C|Blade Dance}}, {{C|Storm of Steel}}, {{C|All for One}}, {{C|Flurry of Blows}}, and {{C|Weave}}.\n** This is especially true when combined with lots of card draw and {{KW|Energy}} generation effects. Cards such as {{C|Bloodletting}}, {{C|Burning Pact}}, {{C|Acrobatics}}, {{C|Calculated Gamble}}, {{C|TURBO}}, {{C|Skim}}, and many [[Watcher]] strategies that rely on repeatedly swapping {{KW|Stance|Stances}} multiple times each turn can be significantly diminished in their usability.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* A choker is a close-fitting necklace worn around the neck as a fashion accessory. They can be made of velvet, like {{R|Velvet Choker}}, but plastic, beads, latex, leather, and precious metals like gold or silver are also used.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Choker</ref>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Day 1 Patch'''\n** Velvet Choker now functions correctly again. (Thanks to riseblader14)\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]}}}}